d it  can be on a smallest local level it can  be on the most you know seemingly  inconsequential sorts of public affairs  but it's just developing those habits of  political association of being in public  and of making your voice heard that I  think are really crucial and I do think  that they are on the rise at least  within the younger cohort right now I  might add that sometimes it takes a  scandal like that to galvanize people  and say we will not let this be the  final word about asian-americans  participation in politics and let's now  have all the more reason to be involved  and I think perhaps that is what's  happening hopefully it is yes  thank you my name is Kay Elia I first  like to applaud the panelists and thank  the committee of 100 for hosting this  conference and as I look around the room  two things stand out there are two  generations the founders of the  committee 100 who made this possible and  also individuals who are the  beneficiaries and who are the the the  future of our community a couple  comments that were made in terms of role  models and in terms of mentors I happen  to work for a large pharmaceutical  company Pfizer and I'm very involved  with our diversity initiative our  internal diversity initiative and we  started out as a minority mentoring  initiative and mentorship in corporate  America is by far I think the most  critical aspect for for advancement and  my plea is as networking goes occurs at  this forum that the individuals who are  in a position to help reach out and  mentor the younger generation I'm  referring to the founders of the  committee 100 my plea is that to the  action step is the follow up of those  who are looking for mentors that if you  have exchanged cards in here for those  people who are in a position to to help  out please respond because without that  the energies that that have that are so  obvious in this room will go to naught  it's very difficult once we go back to  our respective careers to be lost 